// =============================================================================
// 10-fan-out-ratio.kql
// Proves the seven-times per-turn query amplification.
// =============================================================================
//
// RUN THE DISCOVERY QUERIES FIRST
//   00-discover-dependency-types.kql and 01-discover-search-diagnostic-shape.kql.
//   If 01 shows different column names than those used below (for example
//   ResultSignature instead of resultSignature_d, or an absent IndexName_s),
//   substitute the names it reports before trusting this result.
//
// PURPOSE
//   Show, per minute, how many Search queries were issued against how many
//   distinct indexes. The agent has seven knowledge sources attached, and
//   Copilot Studio does not route among knowledge sources by description below
//   25 sources, so every user turn fans out to all seven. This query is the
//   evidence for that claim.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS
//   Every limit in this architecture is multiplied by seven: semantic ranker
//   concurrency, connector call rate, APIM quota, token volume. Capacity
//   purchases defer the failure; reducing fan-out removes it. Quantifying the
//   multiplier is what turns "reduce your fan-out" from advice into a costed
//   decision.
//   Research: .copilot-tracking/research/2026-09-22/ai-search-scaling-206-403-rca-research.md
//             Lines 164-175 (D7, the 25-source threshold), Lines 203-253 (Scenario 1)
//
// TARGET TABLE
//   AzureDiagnostics, ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH".
//
// PREREQUISITES
//   * Search diagnostic setting sending OperationLogs to this workspace.
//   Enable with: scripts/Enable-DiagnosticSettings.ps1
//
// HEALTHY VS UNHEALTHY
//   Expected here : DistinctIndexes settles at 7 and QueriesPerIndex tracks the
//                   user-turn rate closely. That is the fan-out, confirmed.
//   Healthy target: after consolidation or a move to Tools, DistinctIndexes per
//                   turn drops toward 1 or 2 and TotalQueries falls with it.
//   Unhealthy     : TotalQueries spikes while DistinctIndexes stays flat at 7,
//                   which means concurrent turns are stacking on the same seven
//                   indexes. Cross-reference with 12-semantic-capacity-headroom.kql.
//
// NOTE ON THE METRIC
//   QueriesPerIndex is the honest fan-out signal: it is the mean queries landing
//   on each index in the bin. Reading it alongside DistinctIndexes tells you
//   whether volume growth came from more turns or from more indexes per turn.
//
// Derived from Q1b-FanOutRatio,
//   .copilot-tracking/research/2026-09-22/ai-search-scaling-206-403-rca-research.md Lines 276-291
// =============================================================================

let lookback = 24h;
let bucket = 1m;
AzureDiagnostics
| where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
| where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH"
| where OperationName == "Query.Search"
| summarize
    TotalQueries    = count(),
    DistinctIndexes = dcount(IndexName_s),
    QueriesPerIndex = round(count() * 1.0 / todouble(dcount(IndexName_s)), 2)
    by bin(TimeGenerated, bucket)
| render timechart with (ytitle = "Queries and distinct indexes per minute")

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMPANION: per-index profile
// Which of the seven indexes carries the load, and which is the slow one.
// Run this when the chart above shows uneven pressure.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
